desc = """**The world**: Aumadyr ('Aumara's hearth'), a continent of human kingdoms, dwarven mountains, elven volcanoes, necromancer mines and a lawless free city, joined by a stone bridge in the east to the huge continent of the Direplains, home of the Dire. Medieval, with elves ahead (early flintlocks, clockwork, coal engines) and the lost silver marvels of the trolls buried in ruins. The tone is a mix: gritty politics, wars and dangerous roads, full of funny people. Every character is an adult.

**Karma and mana**: karma is the absolute natural law of judgement on all things (Thessa's law, still running though the gods are gone); mortal law is semantics. Every deed has a weight. Mana comes from karma: positive karma is plentiful and gives little mana, negative karma gives far more. Nobody can measure karma faithfully; only trained mages and Ostari sense it, and extremes bend a person's luck. Investigations start when someone's mana grows suspiciously fast; laws differ by nation. Eating someone purely for food is positive karma (still a legal grey zone); eating with ill intent is negative; eating a willing person is positive for both, a gift freely given.

**Magic**: anyone can use magic. Tune your mana by vividly imagining what you want until the mana makes it real, streaming it through a limb or a catalyst (a staff stores more for one cast; silver is best for spell-swords). Everyone has one natural affinity; other kinds cost far more. Overreach means burnout (exhaustion, nosebleeds, fainting). No time magic, and no making mana from nothing (only troll silver can). Resurrection works only very soon after a death and with absolutely no bias, so healers who don't know the dead do it best. Mana is stored in silver (best) and some gems; troll-era silver is 'sick' and drains mana, and the black market sells it as weapons against mages. The gods' old tongue is a spell language.

**The gods**: the pantheon was real and went silent after the trolls fell (Aumara of the land, Ossvar of death, Selune of the moon, Kethrak of the forge, Ylwen of growing things, Varrok of the hunt and the belly, Thessa the Weigher). Ossvar is dead: his corpse lies deep inside Hollowmouth, the cave island in the central bay. Their laws still work, their holy places stir, omens come, and very rarely someone is born or touched with a god's mark and an ability. Lesser gods (their children) exist, a few walking the world in disguise, others bound or hidden. Ancient sapient dragons once served the gods.

**The three moons**: Selune's tide moon (ice magic waxes with it; the Blood Moon is it turning red, when karma swings hardest), the cracked crystalline moon of the Ostari with shards drifting around it like a ring, and a third 'moon' that nobody understands (it is secretly a hole in reality).

**Vore**: all Dire can swallow people (oral and anal); among the other races the women and futas have it as a racial knack (with exceptions among beast races and femboys). Oral, anal, cock vore and unbirth all happen. Digestion is fatal; afterwards come weight gain, burped-up bones and bony scat, or cum loads after cock vore. Outside the Direplains people-eating is illegal almost everywhere (allowed in self-defence or as crude, frowned-upon public executions) and is a dirty open secret (Riftmarket's bellyhouses) and a moral panic at once; it varies a lot by kingdom (see the lore). Many Dire turn bones into armour, tools and money.

**Lewdness**: Dire blunt and crude; elves experimental; dwarves private and intense; goblins mostly for show (they breed through tree seeds); humans vary (Valdrenn lusty, others prim); Seladrak earnest learners; snubs hidden. Female and futa Ostari love giving head with perfect, pillowy lips that leave lip-prints. Festivals include Lysara's Ripening, a breeding-heavy spring day.

**Travel**: horses and carts, beasts of burden (giant lizards, shaggy oxen-beasts, ridden birds), Dire porters and ferries (a little risky: some get hungry), trade roads kept 'safe-ish' by the Hearthwardens, the stone bridge east, and coastal ships that avoid Hollowmouth. Hearthspeak is the common trade tongue. The wilds are dangerous: mana-warped beasts, god-spawned horrors and many monsters that swallow people.

**The Dark Castle**: Vez'nan's castle (built and stolen 300-500 years ago) stands in the dark Brimstone peaks of the southwest under endless Fulgarsoot storms. Vez'nan is long dead, many masters have come and gone, and the Dark Master's seat is vacant now; its minions wait. The castle's hidden hand is in most of the world's politics (spies, debts, 'Ashbrand' goods of unknown origin), groundwork for a slow conquest nobody sees coming.

**Everyone listed is an adult.** Keep every listing exactly as written."""

